[
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Waterfall documents requirements up front in a requirements document and follows a linear sequence where elicitation, analysis, and documentation precede design, making late changes difficult; XP, by contrast, builds to requirements defined at the time and encodes them as test cases, allowing adaptation through iterative releases and emergent requirements.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "The waterfall model presents a very high-level view of what goes on during development, and it suggests to developers the sequence of events they should expect to encounter.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "The waterfall model can be very useful in helping developers lay out what they need to do. Its simplicity makes it easy to explain to customers who are not familiar with software development; it makes explicit which intermediate products are necessary  in  order  to  begin  the  next  stage  of development.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Extreme programming (XP) takes agile requirements processes to the extreme, in that the system is built to the requirements that happen to be defined at the time, with no planning or designing for possible future requirements.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "The forgoes traditional requirements documentation, and instead encodes the requirements as test cases that the eventual implementation must pass.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "as new requirements emerge with use of the system or with better understanding of the problem, they are implemented in subsequent releases of the system.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "This incremental development allows for 'early and continuous delivery of valuable software' (Beck et al. 2004) and accommodates emergent and late-breaking requirements.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ]
    }
]

[
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "In COCOMO II Stage 1, the number of data tables used by a screen helps determine its complexity class via the guidance illustrated in the example where the booking screen may require three data tables, and the screen is then rated as simple according to the application point table, after which Table 3.11 is used to assign a complexity rate (1 for simple screens, 2 for medium screens, and 5 for medium reports). The individual weights (complexity points) are summed to produce the count of new application points (NOPS); for the example, this total equals 9.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "For example,the booking screen may require the use of three data tables:",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Thus, the number of data tables is fewer than four, so we must decide whether we need more than eight views. Since we are likely to need fewer than eight views, we rate the booking screen as 'simple' according to the application point table.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Next, we use Table 3.11 to assign a complexity rate of 1 to simple screens, 2 to medium screens, and 5 to medium reports; a summary of our ratings is shown in Table 3.15.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "We add all the weights in the rightmost column to generate a count of new application points (NOPS): 9.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "The initial effort estimate is then derived by adjusting the NOPS with a productivity rate that depends on developer experience and CASE maturity. Table 3.12 provides the productivity rate (for example, 7 when both experience and CASE maturity are low), and the COCOMO model estimates effort as NOP divided by the productivity rate.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Then the COCOMO model tells us that the estimated effort to build the Piccadilly system is NOP divided by the productivity rate, or 1.29 person-months.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Table 3.12 tells us that the productivity rate for this circumstance is 7.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  }
]

[
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "In a Pipe-and-Filter architecture, data is passed from one independent filter to the next via pipes, with filters transforming the data as it flows and little to no persistent storage between steps.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Pipe-and-Filter: system functionality is achieved by passing input data through a sequence of data-transforming components, called filters , to produce output data. Pipes are connectors that simply transmit data from one filter to  the  next  without  modifying  the  data.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Each  filter  is  an  independent  function  that makes no assumptions about other filters that may be applied to the data.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Unlike the other designs, a data item is no longer stored in any location, but rather flows from one filter to another, being transformed along the way.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "In a Blackboard repository architecture, by contrast, data is stored in a centralized data store (the blackboard) and data-accessing components (knowledge sources) react to its current contents. The blackboard stores the current state of the system's computation and knowledge sources iteratively revise and improve the solution by applying rewriting rules.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "However, the blackboard type of repository (illustrated in Figure 5.8), the dataaccessing components are reactive : they execute in reaction to the current contents of the data store.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "More commonly, the blackboard stores the current state of the system's computation,and knowledge sources detect pieces of the unsolved problem to tackle. For example, in a rule-based system, the current state of the solution is stored in the blackboard, and knowledge sources iteratively revise and improve the solution by applying rewriting rules.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "An important property of this style of architecture is the centralized management of the system's key data. In the data store, we can localize responsibility for storing persistent data, managing concurrent access to the data, enforcing security and privacy policies, and protecting the data against faults (e.g., via backups).",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "A key architectural decision is whether to map data to more than one data store. Distributing or replicating data may improve system performance, but often there are costs: adding complexity, keeping data stores consistent, and reducing security.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ]
    }
]

[
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "In the V-model, verification activities on the right side (unit and integration testing) are used to verify the program design and ensure correctness of the implementation, while system testing verifies the system design; acceptance testing by the customer validates the requirements by tying tests to each specification element and confirms all requirements are implemented before acceptance.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Unit and integration testing addresses the correctness of programs, as we shall see in later chapters.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "The V model suggests that unit and integration testing can also be used to verify the program design.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Similarly, system testing should verify the system design, making sure that all system design aspects are correctly implemented.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Acceptance testing, which is conducted by the customer rather than the developer, validates the requirements by associating a testing step with each element of the specification; this type of testing checks to see that all requirements have been fully implemented before the system is accepted and paid for.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    }
]

[
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "It may be a poor choice when the majority of team members are introverts because the chief programmer team centralizes decision-making with the chief and requires frequent communication with the chief, which may not suit an introvert-heavy group: the text states that \"If most of the team members are introverts, the chief programmer team may not be the best structure for the project.\"",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "If most of the team members are introverts, the chief programmer team may not be the best structure for the project.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "For highly uncertain projects, the recommended alternative is a more democratic approach with a loosely defined hierarchy and open communication: \"On the other hand, when there is much uncertainty involved in a project, a more democratic approach may be better. ... Here, participation in decision making, a loosely defined hierarchy, and the encouragement of open communication can be effective.\"",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "On the other hand, when there is much uncertainty involved in a project, a more democratic approach may be better. For example, if the requirements may change as development proceeds, the project has a degree of uncertainty. Likewise, suppose your customer is building a new piece of hardware to interface with a system; if the exact specification of the hardware is not yet known, then the level of uncertainty is high. Here, participation in decision making, a loosely defined hierarchy, and the encouragement of open communication can be effective.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  }
]

