[
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Parnas tables express conditions as predicates in the column and row headers, and the internal table entries store the possible function results; each internal table entry represents a distinct case in the function's definition, with a table entry of 'X' indicating that the operation is invalid under the specified conditions. The headers are structured to cover all possible combinations of conditions that can affect the calculation of a loaned item's due date. By contrast, a Z specification schema represents pre-conditions and post-conditions in explicit top and bottom sections: the top indicates whether the operation modifies or queries the system state and identifies inputs and outputs, while the bottom specifies the operation's pre-conditions and post-conditions. For operations that modify state, unprimed variables are before and primed variables are after, and post-conditions update state (e.g., Catalogue) to reflect the new values, sometimes using mappings and domain operations.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The column and row headers are predicates used to specify cases, and the internal table entries store the possible function results.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Thus, each internal table entry represents a distinct case in the function's definition.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "A table entry of 'X' indicates that the operation is invalid under the specified conditions;",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Notice how the column and row headers are structured to cover all possible combinations of conditions that can affect the calculation of a loaned item's due date.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The top section of an operation schema indicates whether the operation modifies ( D ) or simply queries ( X ) the system state, and identifies the inputs (?) and outputs (!) of the operation.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The bottom section of an operation schema specifies the operation's pre-conditions and post-conditions.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "In operations that modify the system state, unprimed variables represent variable values before the operation is performed, and primed variables represent values following the operation.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The post-conditions update the Catalogue to include the new Item , and specify that the other library variables do not change value (e.g., the updated value Fine equals the old value Fine ).",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ]
  }
]

[
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "The hot standby redundancy failed because the fault-recovery design assumed failures were independent and limited to hardware; however, software faults are often logical and affect all replicated software copies, so hot standby is not effective for software faults. In Ariane-5, the hot-standby units did not independently avoid the same issue: both inertial reference systems suffered the same overflow error simultaneously.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "The Ariane-5 included a number of redundant components in which both the hardware equipment and the associated software were identical. In most cases, one of the components was supposed to be active; the other was to stay in 'hot standby' mode, ready to become the active component if the current active component failed.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "hot standby redundancy in Ariane-5 is likely to recover only from hardware failures.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "software faults tend to be logical errors, so all copies of a software component have the same set of faults.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "both units suffered the same overflow error, simultaneously.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    }
]

[
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "In a pure layered architecture like OSI, layers provide services to the layer above and act as a client to the layer below, while keeping the details of lower layers hidden.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Layered systems organize the system's software units into layers, each of which provides services to the layer above it and acts as a client to the layer below.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "A pure layered system restricts a layer to access only units within the same layer and the services offered by the interface to the layer immediately below it, to preserve layering.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "In a 'pure' layered system, a software unit in a given layer can access only the other units in the same layer and services offered by the interface to the layer immediately below it.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Because a layer must not access higher-level layers, allowing such access would violate the layering principle and the architecture would no longer be considered layered.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Under no circumstances does a layer access the services offered by a higher-level layer; the resulting architecture would no longer be called layered.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "To minimize impact when modifying a layer, the architecture relies on explicit interface specifications to hide design decisions, thereby reducing visibility into implementation details and limiting coupling.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Interface specifications keep other developers from knowing about and exploiting our design decisions.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "To promote low coupling, we want to keep a unit's interface as small and simple as possible.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "If a developer writes code that depends on how our software is implemented, then the interface between that developer's code and our code has changed: the developer's code now requires more from our software than what is advertised in our software's interface.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ]
    }
]

