[
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Strategically, companies justify the upfront higher cost by planning to reuse assets across multiple related products, aiming to achieve economies of scope rather than treating each product as entirely separate development. As described: \"The products may appear to be quite different from one another, varying in size, quality, features, or price. But they have enough in common to allow the company to take advantage of economies of scope by sharing technologies (e.g., architecture, common parts, test suites, or environments), assembly facilities (e.g., workers, assembly plants), business plans (e.g., budgets, release schedules), marketing strategies and distribution channels, and so on.\"",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "The corporate strategy for designing and developing the related products is based on the reuse of elements of a common product line . The company plans upfront to manufacture and market several related products. Part of the planning process involves deciding how those products will share assets and resources. The products may appear to be quite different from one another, varying in size, quality, features, or price. But they have enough in common to allow the company to take advantage of economies of scope by sharing technologies (e.g., architecture, common parts, test suites, or environments), assembly facilities (e.g., workers, assembly plants), business plans (e.g., budgets, release  schedules), marketing  strategies  and  distribution  channels, and  so  on.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "ROI and long-term profitability are tied to how the product line is scoped and how much of the derived products’ architectures start from the product-line architecture. A product line succeeds when its core asset base is used to supply a significant fraction of each derived product, with the product design then adding only accommodated changes. The less the final derived product has in common with the product line, the more the derived product's development will resemble a completely new (i.e., nonderived) product.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "The less the final derived product has in common with the product line, the more the derived product's development will resemble a completely new (i.e., nonderived) product.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Additionally, the product-line notion is discussed as an assembly-line style process where the core asset base is planned carefully and evolves as the family grows, enabling many components to be adapted from core assets and plugged together rather than built from scratch, which supports scalable ROI through reuse.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Because  the  products  in  the product  family  are  related, the  opportunities for reuse  abound  and  extend  well  beyond  the  reuse  of  code  units. Clements  and\nNorthrop (2002) describe why a number of candidate elements may belong in the core asset base:",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Moreover, the central goal is to ensure that development of derived products resembles an assembly line: many components can be adapted from core assets and then plugged together, while the core asset base is designed and evolves as the product family grows to include new products.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "The family's commonalities are described as a collection of reusable assets (including requirements, designs, code, and test cases), all stored in a core asset base . When developing products in the family, we retrieve assets from the base as needed.As a  result, development  resembles  an  assembly  line: many  of  the  components  can be adapted from components in the core asset base and then plugged together, rather than developing each from scratch. The design of the core asset base is planned carefully, and it evolves as the product family grows to include new products.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ]
    }
]

